Scalp laceration from minor head strike against cabinet door to parietal region
Minor Trauma and Personal Injury
Profuse localized scalp bleeding, Intact underlying calvarium, Normal neurological status with GCS 15

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: MTI-016
Reason for Visit: Acute, profusely bleeding head wound following blunt trauma, requiring urgent closure and neurological assessment.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient and spouse
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage reported a head strike with profuse, continuous bleeding that could not be adequately controlled by the patient. Urgent assessment was required to definitively arrest the hemorrhage, exclude a depressed skull fracture, and perform a comprehensive neurological clearance prior to the next available in-hours consultation.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: I stood up into the cabinet door and my head split open; it won't stop bleeding.
History of Present Illness: Patient reports a linear discontinuity of the scalp extending to the subcutaneous tissue following a low-velocity blunt-force impact against a stationary wooden cabinet door. There is profuse, brisk venous bleeding out of proportion to the wound size, secondary to high scalp vascularity. The patient confirms a total absence of loss of consciousness, amnesia, or altered mental status surrounding the event. There has been no severe recurrent vomiting, intractable headache, or seizure activity post-injury.
Functional Status: Fully independent and mobilizing well.
Social/Safety Concerns: Minor slip hazard created by spilled blood on the kitchen floor; patient’s spouse is actively cleaning.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 142/86 | HR: 90 | Temp: 36.8 | RR: 16 | SpO2: 100% | Wt: 82 Kg
General: Alert, well-perfused, holding a blood-soaked towel to the parietal scalp.
Physical Exam: Scalp examination reveals a 3cm linear laceration with profuse localized scalp bleeding. The wound edges are relatively clean and easily opposable with manual pressure. Exploration of the wound bed with sterile instruments confirms an intact, smooth underlying calvarium without step-off, crepitus, or depression. Neurological status is completely normal with a GCS of 15. Pupils are equal and reactive. Complete absence of clinical signs indicating basilar skull fracture, such as hemotympanum, Battle's sign, or periorbital ecchymosis.
Environmental Observations: Kitchen cabinet door identified as the mechanism. No sharp edges or exposed nails noted.
Medication Reconciliation: No routine anticoagulants.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Scalp laceration to parietal region - Status: Stable
Differentials:

Intracranial hemorrhage - Ruled out due to: GCS 15, absence of vomiting, severe headache, focal neurological deficit, and negative history of loss of consciousness (Canadian CT Head Rule negative).

Depressed skull fracture - Ruled out due to: Direct palpation of the calvarium confirming a smooth, intact contour without step-off.

Arterial laceration (Superficial temporal) - Ruled out due to: Bleeding characterized as dark venous flow easily controlled with direct pressure, rather than pulsatile arterial spurting.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Low

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The presentation of a highly vascular scalp laceration following blunt head trauma necessitated immediate bedside evaluation to rule out underlying traumatic brain injury and definitively manage the hemorrhage. Clinical assessment confidently confirmed an isolated soft tissue injury with an intact skull and a normal neurological baseline, satisfying criteria for safe community management. Urgent after-hours intervention was essential to provide primary wound closure, secure hemostasis, and establish a firm neurological baseline, safely bypassing an unnecessary emergency department visit for a non-contrast CT scan.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Wound irrigated with 500mL sterile saline. Primary closure achieved using tissue adhesive (cyanoacrylate) and steri-strips after securing hemostasis. Tetanus status reviewed and up to date.
Positive Behavioral Change: Apply firm, continuous, direct pressure with a clean cloth for exactly 10 minutes by the clock if minor oozing occurs. This physical pressure overcomes local venous pressure, allowing the natural clotting cascade to permanently seal the microvessels.
Home-Based Services (Optional): None.
DME/Safety (Optional): None.
Follow-up: Routine GP review in 7 days for wound check and removal of any loose steri-strips.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Proceed immediately to the Emergency Department via ambulance if you develop severe worsening headaches, repetitive vomiting, confusion, clumsiness, or double vision.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: Head injuries require a 24-hour observation period as delayed intracranial bleeding can occasionally occur.
Clinical Course: The scalp wound will heal rapidly over 7 days. Mild localized soreness and a small palpable ridge of scar tissue are normal during healing.

SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

Seek emergency medical attention if you become excessively drowsy and cannot be easily woken, or if clear fluid starts leaking from your nose or ears.

PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: Tissue adhesive provides a waterproof, sterile seal that holds the skin edges together without the need for painful sutures in the highly sensitive scalp.
Actionable Steps: Do not wash your hair for 48 hours. When you do wash it, let water run over the wound gently but do not scrub the glued area. Sleep with your head elevated on two pillows tonight to reduce throbbing.

